\title{Why 527 Schedule B is not a matchdb source}
\description{
\code{2024-11-POL-ORGS-SCHED-B.csv} (1.33 GB, 6,936,571 rows) records what a 527
\emph{paid out}: filer \code{EIN} and \code{ORG_NAME} on one side, \code{RECIEPIENT_NAME} plus an
address on the other, with \code{EXPENDITURE_AMOUNT}, \code{EXPENDITURE_PURPOSE} and
\code{EXPENDITURE_DATE}.
}
\details{
\strong{The recipient has no EIN.} That makes the file structurally identical to
990-PF Part XIV grants - a payer identified by EIN, a payee identified only by
a name and address someone typed. It is therefore a \emph{query-side} dataset: the
kind of file you run \strong{against} a disambiguation database, exactly as
\code{pfmatch} runs grants against the reference. It is not a source of
\code{(name -> EIN)} observations, because the EIN on the row belongs to the payer,
not to the name being described.

Ingesting it as a \code{name} source would attach every payee's name to the \emph{payer's}
EIN - aliasing "Hawaii Republican Party" onto a teachers' union PAC. That is
the single most destructive thing that could be done to a disambiguation
reference, and the row shape makes it an easy mistake, which is why the
exclusion is written down rather than left implicit.

Two further reasons it does not belong on the reference side: most payees are
political committees, candidates and vendors rather than exempt organisations;
and \code{RECIPIENT_OCCUPATION} and \code{RECIEPIENT_EMPLOYER} are individual-level
fields, so a large share of rows describe people, who have no place in an
organisational reference.

The file is kept under \verb{sources/query-side/} as a future matching workload.
